\title{Neuro-Symbolic Language Modeling via P\=a\protect\d{n}inian Algebraic Priors: Eliminating the Syntax Search Space in Transformer Architectures}

\author{
\IEEEauthorblockN{Varun Kuruvalli}
\IEEEauthorblockA{
kuruvallivarunjp@gmail.com}
}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Current Large Language Models (LLMs) rely heavily on dense parameter networks and autoregressive attention mechanisms to statistically approximate syntactic structures. For morphologically rich, free-word-order languages like Sanskrit, this probabilistic approach results in extreme computational inefficiencies, high ``token taxes,'' and arbitrary positional dependencies. We propose a novel Neuro-Symbolic Transformer architecture that decouples syntax from semantics. By mapping the generative rules of P\=a\protect\d{n}ini's \textit{A\d{s}\protect\d{t}\=adhy\=ay\={\i}} to deterministic mathematical functions, we generate a highly sparse, mathematically verified Adjacency Matrix ($M$). Injecting this matrix directly into the attention mechanism as a structural prior forces the network to route purely along valid K\=araka (dependency) pathways. This architecture fundamentally reduces the computational complexity of syntactic attention from $O(N^2)$ to $O(N \cdot k)$, freeing the neural parameters to model pure semantics and exponentially reducing the data required for model convergence.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Neuro-Symbolic AI, Large Language Models, P\=a\protect\d{n}inian Grammar, Sanskrit Computational Linguistics, Sparse Attention
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
The prevailing paradigm in Natural Language Processing (NLP) assumes that, given sufficient scale, neural networks will internally deduce the grammatical rules of any language. Consequently, modern foundational models execute dense Self-Attention computations across full $N \times N$ sequence grids, continuously updating Query ($Q$) and Key ($K$) projections to separate valid syntactic connections from impossible ones \cite{efficientllm}.

While this brute-force statistical approach is effective for positionally rigid languages like English, it struggles against the algebraic, highly inflected nature of languages like Sanskrit. Standard tokenizers (such as Byte-Pair Encoding and SentencePiece) shatter morphological roots based on statistical frequency, leading to severe ``token explosions'' when processing Indic languages \cite{token_efficient}. 



Furthermore, the reliance on Rotary Positional Embeddings (RoPE) arbitrarily penalizes Sanskrit's native free-word-order architecture. This paper introduces a paradigm shift: a Neuro-Symbolic bridge that completely offloads syntax to a deterministic, rule-based computational engine inspired by P\=a\protect\d{n}ini's 2,000-year-old algorithmic framework, the \textit{A\d{s}\protect\d{t}\=adhy\=ay\={\i}} \cite{desai2026, kak_paninian}. By pre-solving the structural mathematics of the language, the neural network acts exclusively as a semantic reasoning engine operating within mathematically bounded pathways.

\section{Related Work}
The translation of P\=a\protect\d{n}inian rules into computational formalisms is a mature field within Sanskrit Computational Linguistics (SCL). Early systems focused on constructing deterministic morphological analyzers and parsers \cite{scl_research, astadhyayi_parser}. Recent frameworks, such as Vidyut-Prakriy\=a \cite{vidyut_prakriya} and the Sanskrit Heritage Engine, process Sandhi (euphonic combinations) and Sam\=asa (compounds) with mathematical precision.

Parallel to this, Computational Paninian Grammar (CPG) frameworks have been developed to map syntactic dependencies (K\=araka) in Indian languages. However, these tools operate primarily as standalone symbolic engines or preprocessing pipelines. The recent Sanskrit Linguistic Intelligence Pipeline (SLIP) \cite{slip_ieee} successfully injected morphological knowledge into Neural Machine Translation (NMT) architectures, achieving significant F1 score improvements. Yet, SLIP remains a pipeline augmentation rather than a fundamental redesign of the core attention mechanism.

Crucially, a literature review reveals a phenomenon of ``lexical misattribution.'' Several deep learning models bear the name ``Panini''---such as a Bangla grammatical error correction Transformer \cite{bangla_panini} and a facial restoration GAN \cite{panini_net}---but none inherently integrate the \textit{A\d{s}\protect\d{t}\=adhy\=ay\={\i}}'s algorithmic mechanisms into their core topology. This highlights a vast, unaddressed research gap in actual neuro-symbolic LLM design.

\section{The P\=a\protect\d{n}inian Neuro-Symbolic Architecture}
The proposed architecture bifurcates language processing into two parallel streams: a discrete symbolic track handling syntax, and a continuous neural track handling semantics. These tracks intersect exclusively at the attention computation and decoding layers.



\subsection{Phase 1: Deterministic Morphological Ingestion}
Standard sub-word tokenization is replaced by a Finite State Transducer (FST). This module strictly resolves phonological combinations to yield pure morphological tokens: roots (\textit{prak\d{r}ti}) and affixes (\textit{pratyaya}). This guarantees absolute structural integrity before vectorization \cite{token_efficient}.

\subsection{Phase 2: Parallel State Processing}
\textbf{Track A: The Symbolic Engine (Syntax):} The FST output is parsed by a deterministic rule engine. Tokens are tagged with grammatical metadata (e.g., \textit{Puru\d{s}a}, \textit{Vacana}, \textit{Vibhakti}). The engine evaluates valid K\=araka relationships to generate a sparse Adjacency Matrix $M \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times N}$. If token $i$ can grammatically govern token $j$, $M_{i,j} = 0$. If mathematically impossible, $M_{i,j} = -\infty$.



\textbf{Track B: Position-Agnostic Embeddings (Semantics):} Simultaneously, tokens are mapped to dense vectors $X \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times d}$. Explicit positional encodings (e.g., RoPE) are intentionally omitted to natively support free word-order.

\subsection{Phase 3: Sparse P\=a\protect\d{n}inian Attention}
This phase represents the core computational optimization. Self-attention is strictly routed by Matrix $M$:

\begin{equation}
\text{Attention}(Q, K, V) = \text{softmax}\left(\text{Sparse}\left(\frac{QK^T}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right) + M\right)V
\end{equation}



Instead of computing the dense dot-product grid, custom hardware kernels utilize $M$ as a fetch-mask. If $M_{i,j} = -\infty$, the key vector $K_j$ is bypassed entirely, slashing floating-point operations (FLOPs).

\subsection{Phase 4 \& 5: Semantic Maturation and Constrained Decoding}
Contextualized vectors pass through Feed-Forward Networks (FFN). Because syntax is pre-solved, these parameters are strictly allocated to higher-order semantic logic. During autoregressive generation, a dynamic mask $M_{\text{decode}}$ is applied to the final logits before the softmax function, completely eliminating the probability of grammatical hallucinations.



\section{Theoretical Efficiency Gains}
The integration of algebraic priors yields three transformative advantages:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Computational Complexity:} Complexity scales linearly relative to $k$ (the average number of grammatically valid connections per token) rather than quadratically ($O(N^2) \rightarrow O(N \cdot k)$) \cite{mit2026, pattekar2026}.
    \item \textbf{Parameter Decoupling:} The network requires significantly fewer parameters in the FFN layers, as syntax enforcement is entirely offloaded.
    \item \textbf{Data Efficiency:} By injecting structural ground truth directly into the attention mechanism, the model bypasses the need to statistically deduce grammar, steeply accelerating the gradient descent curve and drastically reducing the required corpus size \cite{sanskrit_advancing_ai}.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}
This paper introduces a Neuro-Symbolic framework that maps the algebraic rules of P\=a\protect\d{n}inian grammar directly onto the self-attention mechanisms of Transformer architectures. By replacing statistical brute-force syntax approximation with deterministic mathematical routing, the AI community can construct the next generation of highly efficient, sovereign language models.

